.\" ufsbe.8 — manual page for the ufsbe FUSE3 UFS filesystem daemon
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 11, 2026
.Dt UFSBE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ufsbe
.Nd FUSE3 UFS1/UFS2 filesystem daemon with B-epsilon tree
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar special
.Ar mountpoint
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a userspace filesystem daemon that mounts a UFS1 or UFS2 block
device or disk image on
.Ar mountpoint
using the FUSE3 kernel interface.
.Pp
.Nm
supports:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
UFS1 and UFS2 on-disk formats created by
.Xr newfs 8 .
.It
Soft Updates
.Pq softdep .
.It
Soft Update Journaling
.Pq SUJ :
the journal is replayed automatically when the filesystem was not
cleanly unmounted.
.It
A write-back LRU buffer cache with
.Dv B_BARRIER
ordering for soft-updates correctness.
.It
A B-epsilon tree block map that replaces traditional direct and
indirect block pointers, yielding significantly higher random-I/O
throughput.
.It
A B-epsilon tree directory index that provides O(log N) name lookup
using a 64-bit FNV1a hash.
The on-disk
.Vt struct direct
linear layout is preserved for compatibility with
.Xr fsck_ffs 8
and the kernel UFS driver.
.El
.Pp
The
.Xr fusefs 4
kernel module must be loaded before
.Nm
is invoked.
See
.Sx SETUP .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl o Ar options
Comma-separated mount options passed to FUSE3.
Recognised options include:
.Bl -tag -width "allow_other" -compact
.It Cm ro
Mount read-only.
.It Cm allow_other
Allow users other than the mounting user to access the filesystem.
Requires
.Xr sysctl 8
.Va vfs.usermount
to be set to 1, or the mount to be performed by root.
.It Cm default_permissions
Enable kernel-side permission checking.
.El
.El
.Sh SETUP
Load the fusefs kernel module:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload fusefs
.Ed
.Pp
To load it at boot, add the following line to
.Pa /boot/loader.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fusefs_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.Ss On-disk format
.Nm
reads and writes standard UFS2 superblocks and cylinder groups using
.Xr libufs 3 .
The on-disk format is identical to that produced by
.Xr newfs 8 .
.Ss B-epsilon tree block map
Each inode's block map is stored in a persistent B-epsilon tree
(Brodal & Fagerberg 1996, Bender et al. 2007).
Tree root block numbers are stored in the
.Va di_extb[0]
and
.Va di_extb[1]
fields of the
.Vt ufs2_dinode
structure; these fields are unused by the kernel for inodes without
extended attributes.
A filesystem that has been used with
.Nm
can be mounted by the kernel UFS driver afterwards:
the kernel will treat
.Va di_extb
as zero and rebuild its own block map from the direct and indirect
block pointers \(em which are absent in the B-epsilon representation.
Running
.Xr fsck_ffs 8
after a kernel mount will recover the filesystem fully.
.Ss Soft Update Journaling
The SUJ circular log is detected from the superblock
.Va FS_SUJ
flag.
On mount,
.Nm
replays any committed but unapplied journal records before
making the filesystem available.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Mount a UFS2 partition:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ufsbe /dev/ada0s1a /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Mount a disk image read-only:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ufsbe -o ro /path/to/disk.img /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Mount via
.Xr mount 8
using the
.Xr mount_ufsbe 8
wrapper:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# mount -t ufsbe /dev/ada0s1a /mnt
.Ed
.Pp
Mount via
.Xr mount_fusefs 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# mount_fusefs auto /mnt ufsbe /dev/ada0s1a
.Ed
.Pp
Unmount:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# umount /mnt
.Ed
.Sh FSTAB ENTRY
To mount automatically at boot, add an entry to
.Xr fstab 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/dev/ada0s1a  /mnt  ufsbe  rw  0  0
.Ed
.Pp
Note that FUSE filesystems are not mounted during early boot
.Pq before the FUSE module and userland daemon are available .
Place the entry after the root filesystem has been set up, or use
.Xr rc.conf 5
.Va late_mounts .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libufs 3 ,
.Xr fusefs 4 ,
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr fsck_ffs 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr mount_fusefs 8 ,
.Xr mount_ufsbe 8 ,
.Xr newfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An The ufsbe authors
.Sh CAVEATS
Hard links, extended attributes, and ACLs are not currently supported.
Triple-indirect block addresses are not supported in the B-epsilon
block map path; files requiring more than approximately
.Pq nindir^2 + nindir + 12
blocks will return
.Er ENOSPC .
.Sh BUGS
The B-epsilon tree roots are stored in
.Va di_extb[] ,
which conflicts with extended attribute storage if the kernel later
enables per-inode extended attributes on the same filesystem.
